# Build context = the sources cargo needs, and nothing else.
#
# `target/`, `data/`, `examples/`, `ts/` and `prototype/` are the five that
# matter. `data/` alone is ~600 MB of live mainnet + Sepolia feed and databases,
# and shipping any of them into the context would make every build upload them
# to the daemon.
#
# `examples/` is excluded for a second reason on top of its 515 MB (a vendored
# starknet-privacy clone plus node_modules): the demo scripts write operator key
# material to `examples/*/keystore/`, gitignored but still present on disk. The
# `COPY . .` below would bake that into the *builder* stage's layer, which is
# recoverable with `--target builder`, a buildx cache export, or `docker save`,
# even though the runtime stage copies only the binary. Nothing under
# `examples/` is a cargo workspace member, so the build never needs it.

target/
data/
examples/
.git/
.gitignore
.env
*.log

# The TypeScript halves of the project. Neither is a cargo workspace member and
# no Rust source references a path under either, so `cargo build -p
# strk20-indexerd` never opens them — but both carry a committed `node_modules/`
# and committed build output (`ts/demo/dist/`, replay fixtures), which is 133 MB
# uploaded to the daemon on every build for nothing. Added after `ts/` landed;
# the list above predates it.
ts/
prototype/

# Docs and notes are not compiled.
docs/
README.md
LICENSE

# Not excluded, on purpose:
#   Cargo.lock  — the build runs `--locked` against it
#   crates/     — every workspace member's manifest must exist for `-p` to
#                 resolve the workspace, even though only `strk20-indexerd`
#                 is compiled
#   fixtures/   — only referenced from `#[cfg(test)]` code, so the release
#                 build never opens it, but at 44 KB it is cheaper to keep
#                 than to have `cargo test` inside the image fail to compile

# Anything docker-related is read by the daemon, not copied into the image.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
